--TEST--
`is`, `instanceof` and `as` ask one question about a `Core` value and agree on the answer — `rule:types/type-test`, `rule:types/class-reference-sites`, `rule:types/conversion`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// All three read the same descriptor: the process-wide one
// `nvs_stdlib::class_descriptors` publishes for a `Core` class with instances.
// `is` and `instanceof` answer `bool`, `as` answers the instance or throws, so
// what differs between them is what they do with the answer and never the
// answer itself.
mixed $date = Core\Time\Date::at(2026, 9, 17);

echo ($date is Core\Time\Date) as string, "|\n";
echo ($date instanceof Core\Time\Date) as string, "|\n";
echo ($date as Core\Time\Date)->format("yyyy-MM-dd"), "|\n";

// And they agree on `false` for a class the value is not, one spelling at a
// time: a `Core` class answers for itself alone, so a sibling is no closer
// than a declared class is.
mixed $uri = Core\Uri::parse("https://example.test/a");
echo ($uri is Core\Time\Date) as string, "|\n";
echo ($uri instanceof Core\Time\Date) as string, "|\n";
try {
    Core\Time\Date $wrong = $uri as Core\Time\Date;
    echo "converted|\n";
} catch (Throwable $e) {
    echo "threw|\n";
}

// Both tests narrow their subject on the true edge, so the member call inside
// either branch needs no conversion at all (`rule:types/narrowing`).
if ($uri is Core\Uri) {
    echo $uri->host(), "|\n";
}
if ($uri instanceof Core\Uri) {
    echo $uri->scheme(), "|\n";
}
--EXPECT--
1|
1|
2026-09-17|
|
|
threw|
example.test|
https|
